\title{The Greedy Anti-Fibonacci Sequence:\\ A Sum-Avoiding Rule and Its Exact Structure}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-03}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The Fibonacci recurrence $F(n+1) = F(n) + F(n-1)$ binds consecutive terms together by
addition and drives the ratio of consecutive terms to the golden ratio $\varphi = (1+\sqrt5)/2$.
We study its natural antagonist: the \emph{greedy anti-Fibonacci sequence}, obtained by starting
at $1$ and repeatedly appending the smallest positive integer not yet used that is \emph{not}
the sum of two consecutive earlier terms. We prove that this self-avoiding construction has a
strikingly simple closed form: its $k$-th term (0-indexed) is $A(k) = \lfloor(3k+2)/2\rfloor$,
and its value set is exactly the positive integers not divisible by $3$. The avoided values --
the consecutive sums -- are exactly the positive multiples of $3$. From a single identity,
$A(k)+A(k+1) = 3(k+1)$, we derive the sequence's full structure: a characterization of its terms,
the anti-Fibonacci avoidance property, greedy minimality, and the true asymptotics. The sequence
grows \emph{linearly} with $A(n)/n \to 3/2$ (term density $2/3$); its consecutive ratio converges
to $1$; and its avoided set has density $1/3$. In particular the sequence avoids the golden ratio
in the strongest sense: the very ratio that tends to $\varphi$ for Fibonacci tends to $1$ here.
We also correct a common misattribution: the frequently quoted quadratic list
$1,1,2,4,7,11,16,\dots$ consists of the lazy-caterer numbers $1+\binom{n}{2}$, which do
\emph{not} satisfy the sum-avoidance property and form a genuinely different object.
\end{abstract}

